WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.863 --> 00:00:02.390
- [Instructor] In the year 2000

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:05.660
a wealthy Bostonian named Julian West

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.140
woke up from a very long nap.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.320
He had fallen asleep in the year 1887.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.270
The United States in the year 2000

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.300
was very different from
the Gilded Age he knew.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.880
It was a utopian society
where there was no poverty

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.840
no labor strikes, no pollution.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.160
His new friends in the
future explained to him

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.950
how this society worked.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.740
There was no private
property and no money.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.570
Everyone worked at the job
they were most suited for

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:37.860
and received their fair
share of the national wealth

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.810
on a credit card that they
could use to buy necessities.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:44.110
This is the plot of Edward Bellamy's

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.590
1888 novel "Looking Backward."

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.570
It was a bestseller of its
day but today we remember it

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.530
not only for its
predictions about the future

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.740
but as an example of how
thinkers of the time period

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.970
explored the burning
question of the Gilded Age.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:04.110
Was it possible to have a
modern industrial society

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.880
without great inequalities of wealth?

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.810
In the late 19th century the
rapid changes in American life

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:13.750
stemming from the rise
of industrial capitalism

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.410
caused a great deal of concern.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.890
The United States prided
itself on being different

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.200
from the countries of Europe

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.330
where the inequality
between the aristocracy

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:27.230
and the working class caused
strife and revolution.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.670
But industrialization had brought

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:34.200
both millionaires and
impoverished millions

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.300
to the United States,
packing them in to cities

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.540
where mansions sat side-by-side
with filthy tenements.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:46.540
Suddenly, the New World looked
a lot more like the old world

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.590
and so people at the time wondered

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:52.380
was inequality an inevitable by-product

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.100
of advancing society?

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.350
Those who answered yes were
called Social Darwinists

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.480
who thought that the
survival of the fittest

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.270
would weed out the weak and
improve society overall.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.630
We'll talk more about
Social Darwinism elsewhere

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.820
but in this video I wanna concentrate

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.920
on those who believe that it
was possible to have a society

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.710
that was both modern and equitable.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:18.900
During the Gilded Age there
were a number of reformers

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.990
and reform movements that
attempted to solve the problems

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:26.650
posed by urban and industrial life.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.580
So let's talk about some
of the ways that reformers

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:33.500
attempted to respond to the
inequalities of the Gilded Age.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.760
One was to suggest new economic systems

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.110
for the United States.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:39.640
For example, you might have noticed

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.120
that in Edward Bellamy's utopian society

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.880
there was no private property

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.260
and the national wealth
was equally shared.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.060
In fact, what he was
suggesting was socialism

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.410
a system in which the government,
not private individuals

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.630
owns economic enterprises.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:00.410
Bellamy carefully avoided
saying the word socialism

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.740
which was associated with anarchists

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:04.810
and immigrant radicals

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.560
but he portrayed it as the ultimate remedy

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.280
to all problems in the country.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.030
Bellamy's work influenced many reformers

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.980
including the labor activist
and socialist leader

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.620
Eugene V. Debs who ran for president

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.520
on a socialist platform five times.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.080
Another popular suggestion of the time

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.690
was the single tax which
was proposed by Henry George

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.970
in his book "Progress and Poverty."

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.400
George's solution to wealth inequality

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.020
was to replace all other
taxes with a single high tax

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.360
on the value of land.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.430
He believed that the revenue from this tax

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:41.263
would be enough to pay

00:03:41.263 --> 00:03:44.110
for all necessary government services.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:47.730
One of George's many
admirers was Jacob Riis.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.260
He was a social reformer who published

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.000
a photo expose of tenement
life in New York City

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.300
called "How the Other Half Lives."

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.710
Riis was one of the first muckrakers

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.860
whose chosen method of
combating social problems

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:03.690
was to shine a light on them.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:07.460
His images of dangerous
living conditions in tenements

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:10.770
led to laws which
regulated building safety.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.720
Other muckrakers targeted
the corruption in industry

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.040
like Ida Tarbell, who wrote a history

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:19.650
of the Standard Oil Company

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:22.660
that exposed its unscrupulous practices.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:27.120
Some focused on the unjust
treatment of racial minorities.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.080
In 1881, Helen Hunt
Jackson published a book

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.640
called "A Century of Dishonor"

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:36.240
which discussed the mistreatment, violence

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.840
and broken treaties that
indigenous Americans had faced

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.910
at the hands of the U.S.
government and white settlers.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.300
Journalist Ida B. Wells campaigned

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.080
against the lynching of
black men in the South.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.500
In addition to campaigning

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.520
against economic and social inequality

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.370
many Gilded Age reformers
attempted to remedy the problems

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.750
befalling cities and their residents.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:02.020
The most famous of these was
the settlement house movement.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:04.410
Settlement houses were community centers

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:06.230
based in immigrant neighborhoods

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:09.570
where newcomers could learn
English, get job skills

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:13.240
attain childcare and
find a range of services

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:16.300
that helped them adapt
to life in urban America.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:20.950
Jane Addams founded Hull
House in Chicago in 1889

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:22.300
and many other reformers

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:25.060
founded settlement houses in other cities.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.760
Similarly some churches of the time period

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.300
began to emphasize that confronting

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.940
contemporary social problems
and helping the poor

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.500
were the embodiment of the
teachings of Christianity.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.830
This Social Gospel
movement as it was known

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:44.190
led to the establishment
of missions in urban areas

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.510
and churches opened libraries

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:49.900
gymnasiums and classrooms for public use.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:52.020
Some reformers focused their energies

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.940
on the physical setting of cities

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.940
believing that the squalor of
dirty streets and tenements

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:01.560
depressed people and
encouraged moral decay.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:02.831
The City Beautiful movement

00:06:02.831 --> 00:06:05.950
works to incorporate parks

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:09.100
inspiring architecture and good design

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.740
into American cities.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.580
Frederick Law Olmsted designed
Central Park in New York City

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.930
to be a respite from the urban jungle

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:20.110
and City Beautiful architects
like Daniel Burnham

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:24.380
created monumental spaces and
buildings in Washington D.C.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:27.370
These spaces were supposed
to inspire harmony

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:30.830
order and civic virtue in society.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:34.790
One aspect of Gilded Age reform
you may have noticed by now

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:38.170
is that a large number
of reformers were women.

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:42.260
Jane Addams, Ida B. Wells and Ida Tarbell

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:44.960
all placed prominent
roles in the movement.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.990
After the Civil War a growing
number of middle class women

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:52.280
went to college and these
active and educated women

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.960
began looking for work and
meaning outside of the home.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.850
Many white, middle- and upper-class women

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:01.670
joined clubs dedicated to social reforms

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:05.180
and they argued that
women's traditional role

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:07.070
of keeping their homes clean

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.670
and the people within
them upstanding and moral

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.080
also extended to their communities

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.810
which they called municipal housekeeping.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:17.830
The Women's Christian
Temperance Union, for example

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:21.300
became the Gilded Age's
largest female organization

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.100
with more than 150,000 members.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:28.420
Led by Frances Willard, the
WCTU grew from its roots

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:31.600
in opposing the sale and
consumption of alcohol

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.100
to advocate for policy
solutions to social problems

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.600
ranging from prison reform
to domestic violence.

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:42.320
The WCTU also campaigned
for women's suffrage.

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:46.530
In 1890 the two major women's
suffrage organizations

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.600
which had been at odds with each other

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:52.450
since the passage of the 15th
Amendment reunited to form

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:56.410
the National American
Woman Suffrage Association.

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:57.820
Their efforts would lead

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:00.640
to the growth of woman
suffrage at the state level

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.840
and later, with the help of
the National Woman's Party

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:07.553
the passage of the 19th Amendment
to the U.S. Constitution.

